WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.770
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.070
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.350
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.610
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.430
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:19.410
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:20.570
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:24.260
That's us.

9
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:25.020
Hey there.

10
00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:25.980
We are here.

11
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.400
We are here, and we're excited because today we're talking about Hot Girl.

12
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:35.360
Hot Girl Season 1, Episode 6, written by Mindy Kaling and directed by Amy Heckerling.

13
00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.500
I was really excited to meet Amy Heckerling.

14
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:41.140
You know, growing up, guys, I might be a child of the 70s.

15
00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.440
No judgment, the oldie sitting here.

16
00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:48.060
No, and she directed Fast Times at Ridgemont High and Clueless.

17
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:48.800
Clueless.

18
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:49.340
Clueless.

19
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.140
So I was excited.

20
00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.920
I remember there was a bit of a buzz.

21
00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:53.280
Yeah, there was a little bit of a buzz.

22
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:53.800
That she was coming in.

23
00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:54.660
We were very excited.

24
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.280
I didn't know how to make small talk with her.

25
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:56.820
And guess what?

26
00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:57.480
I failed.

27
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.860
All right, Jenna, hit us with a synopsis.

28
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.760
All right, synopsis of this episode.

29
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.360
Michael allows a woman selling purses to set up in the conference room for the day,

30
00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.780
and Michael and Dwight compete for her attention.

31
00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:11.300
Very simple.

32
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:12.200
Very simple.

33
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:12.700
And yet.

34
00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:14.320
So loaded for Pam.

35
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.860
This was really a big Pam episode.

36
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.060
It really was.

37
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.080
So should we start with some fast facts then?

38
00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.220
I know you want to.

39
00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:21.720
You know.

40
00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.960
My fast fact, number one, this is the first episode written by Mindy Kaling.

41
00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.640
Yes, Mindy Kaling's so smart, so funny.

42
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.180
And she was one of our most prolific writers on The Office.

43
00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.300
She wrote a total of, Jenna, ready?

44
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:33.820
What?

45
00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.340
22 episodes.

46
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:36.120
Wow.

47
00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:36.860
I know.

48
00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.860
That's like she wrote an entire season of The Office.

49
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:40.200
I know.

50
00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.700
Of our nine, because the season's usually about 22, 24 episodes.

51
00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:45.700
I have a fan question about Mindy.

52
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:46.240
All right.

53
00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:47.100
This is from Bear.

54
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:47.860
Hi, Bear.

55
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.460
Yes, Bear asked, this episode was written by Mindy Kaling.

56
00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.620
What was it like to work with her as a writer versus an actor?

57
00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.260
Oh, Bear, good question.

58
00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:56.860
I know.

59
00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:57.940
You know what?

60
00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:03.160
I loved when Mindy was the writer of the episode, because the writer of the episode

61
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:08.720
on our show would stay on set all week and produce their episode with the director.

62
00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.160
So they kind of worked very side by side with the director.

63
00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.800
Yes, and they were sort of the liaison back to the writer's room.

64
00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:15.240
I love that our show did this.

65
00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:16.380
Not all shows do that.

66
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.460
And I really think you benefit from having the writer of the episode on the set with you.

67
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.800
Well, Mindy, it was so easy to make Mindy laugh.

68
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:27.660
She was so ready to love everything you were doing.

69
00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.600
You were filled with such confidence when Mindy was on the set.

70
00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:34.920
And she also was really good on the spot coming up with new jokes, because that was another

71
00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.760
thing that the writer of the episode would do.

72
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.900
They would pitch you improvisations on the spot.

73
00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:41.400
Yeah.

74
00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:45.620
And Mindy, for me, as someone with an improv background, I loved being able to pitch to

75
00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:46.760
Mindy because she's so smart.

76
00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.780
So I could say, hey, Mindy, could I try this?

77
00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.100
And then she would just instantly be like, oh, that'd be great.

78
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:52.980
Or how about you do this?

79
00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.080
Like, she was such a wonderful, creative collaborator.

80
00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.880
I always felt like Mindy was such a big advocate for the character of Pam as well.

81
00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.360
I think she was in the writer's room pitching storylines for Pam.

82
00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:06.540
And this was a very Pam-centered episode, as we said.

83
00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.240
Pam has feelings.

84
00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:09.520
She has a lot of feelings.

85
00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.160
Pam has a lot of feelings.

86
00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:11.840
She does.

87
00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.240
This leads me into my second fastback.

88
00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:19.360
I was texting with Mindy about this episode in preparation, and she told me something.

89
00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:19.960
What?

90
00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.960
The original title of this episode was Purse Girl.

91
00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:23.760
Oh.

92
00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:25.440
Do you kind of remember that?

93
00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:29.800
I actually do, because I feel like I have referred to this as Purse Girl a few times,

94
00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:30.620
because it's in my brain.

95
00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:30.940
Yeah.

96
00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.880
She also told me that her original draft of this was 39 pages.

97
00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:36.120
Wow.

98
00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:41.380
Which is a lot, because usually the number of pages that a script is, that's how many minutes

99
00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:42.400
of screen time it is.

100
00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:47.780
So her script, if we did it exactly as written, would have been 39 minutes, which we would

101
00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:48.680
not have been able to air.

102
00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.420
I really wish we could have done that, and I wish we could have filmed all 40 pages of

103
00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:53.520
that instead of 22.

104
00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:54.460
22 minutes.

105
00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.860
I remember they used to have those very long table drafts, and then they would cut a bunch

106
00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.520
of stuff out, and they would make something they called the candy bag.

107
00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:01.820
Yeah.

108
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.380
And those were all of the cut scenes, or they were additional takes on scenes.

109
00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:12.380
So whatever talking head wound up in the script, there would be five different takes on that

110
00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:13.880
talking head in the candy bag.

111
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:17.580
And so we would sit down to do our talking heads, and they would say, all right, well,

112
00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:21.100
we're going to do the scripted one, but then we're going to do three of them from the candy

113
00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:21.340
bag.

114
00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:26.120
Oh, I remember going to do a talking head, and they handed me three pages of alts, like

115
00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:26.800
right in the minute.

116
00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:27.540
I'm like, oh, right.

117
00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:27.920
Okay.

118
00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.300
I'm going to have to have a photographic memory.

119
00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:31.820
You have to really be on your toes.

120
00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:33.080
I think that's something that-

121
00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:34.600
It kept us on our toes, for sure.

122
00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:35.580
I loved it.

123
00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:36.420
I loved it, too.

124
00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:40.460
And then they used to let me play around and sort of put my spin on it, too, which was

125
00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:40.920
always fun.

126
00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:42.600
All right, Fisher, what else you got?

127
00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:43.160
All right.

128
00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:45.440
I think it's time to talk about Amy AdaMs.

129
00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:46.020
Okay.

130
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.520
I have a lot of note cards here in front of me.

131
00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:49.120
I see.

132
00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.160
And a lot of them have the words Amy or Amy Adams on them.

133
00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:52.260
Okay.

134
00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.060
You tell me when you're ready, because I've got a note card ready to go.

135
00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.020
Well, this is our third fast fact.

136
00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:57.380
Okay.

137
00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:59.840
Amy Adams was the purse girl.

138
00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.460
Mindy told me that Amy was her favorite from the very beginning.

139
00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:07.080
This is who she wanted to cast as the purse girl from her audition.

140
00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:12.060
But something that I think people don't realize is that Amy Adams wasn't Amy Adams when she

141
00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:13.200
was cast as the purse girl.

142
00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:15.720
She became Amy Adams later.

143
00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:20.480
And that's an interesting story, actually, because we filmed this episode in the fall of

144
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.160
2004, but they didn't start airing until the spring of 2005.

145
00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:30.700
And that fall, she was nominated for an Academy Award for her role in Junebug.

146
00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:31.000
Right.

147
00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.680
When she did this episode of The Office, no one had seen Junebug yet, because Junebug

148
00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.660
came out in August of 2005.

149
00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:37.080
Yes.

150
00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:41.680
And I remember her first day on set, we were talking, and I was like, how are things going?

151
00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:42.920
She's like, well, you know, it's good.

152
00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:47.760
I mean, I was on a show on CBS called Dr. Vegas, but they let me go.

153
00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:49.080
Oh, that's right.

154
00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:50.780
She was like, I kind of got fired.

155
00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:51.540
And I was like, no.

156
00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:52.300
And she was like, yeah.

157
00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.600
And I was really down, and I was bummed out.

158
00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:59.600
But then, you know, I did this little indie movie called Junebug, and I feel so good about

159
00:05:59.600 --> 00:05:59.780
it.

160
00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.280
And it's going to come out later in the year.

161
00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:02.740
And I was like, oh, that sounds great.

162
00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:03.340
Yeah.

163
00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:05.020
Let me build you up, Amy AdaMs.

164
00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.180
Amy, you're going to be just fine.

165
00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:09.100
And she was.

166
00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:09.640
And she was.

167
00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:12.040
But Amy is one of the nicest people.

168
00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:14.140
She's so, so sweet.

169
00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:15.620
Oh, I loved Amy.

170
00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:17.800
It was so fun working with her.

171
00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:23.220
We had a lot of questions from fans asking if we planned to bring this character back

172
00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:24.260
after we shot it.

173
00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:26.200
Well, we didn't think we were going to have a show.

174
00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:26.660
Right.

175
00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:28.740
There were no real plans to bring her back.

176
00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:32.140
But then when the show got picked up, they wanted to bring her back.

177
00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:35.160
But now she was Academy Award nominated Amy AdaMs.

178
00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:38.840
I was like, well, I hope we can get her back.

179
00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:39.380
And we did.

180
00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:40.800
She came back for two more episodes.

181
00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:41.400
Yes.

182
00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.800
And she was such a great foil, too, for Pam and Jim and all of that.

183
00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:47.240
I loved watching that play out.

184
00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:52.020
Not only was Amy Adams super nice on set and just like such a pleasure to work with, but

185
00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:55.420
I don't know, probably like two years later, I ran into her.

186
00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:57.460
I had a friend visiting from Alabama.

187
00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:00.280
And you guys, I don't ever go out.

188
00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:01.620
So my friend was coming in town.

189
00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:03.820
And I was like, oh, I'm going to take her somewhere fancy.

190
00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.680
That hotel, Chateau Marmont, that's fancy.

191
00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:07.420
We'll go there.

192
00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:08.500
Because I just had it in my head.

193
00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:10.180
Yeah, like the Hollywood fancy place to eat.

194
00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:10.840
It was a fancy place.

195
00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:11.080
Yeah.

196
00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:12.720
I mean, it's like half an hour from my house.

197
00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:13.900
It was such a like haul.

198
00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:16.080
But I was like, no, I'll take my friend from Alabama there.

199
00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:22.980
So we went and we were having dinner and Amy Adams was at a table and she sent over two

200
00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:24.820
glasses of champagne to my friend and I.

201
00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:27.180
And I was like, they were like, oh, this is so classy.

202
00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.280
Classy move, Amy AdaMs.

203
00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:29.980
And I was like, who's this from?

204
00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:31.680
They were like, it's from Amy AdaMs.

205
00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:34.100
And she waved and then she came over and we chatted.

206
00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:35.880
Amy Adams is just a class act.

207
00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:40.220
I have a funny Amy Adams story about how people think that we look alike.

208
00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:43.900
So she and I were both at this work party thing.

209
00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.200
I can't remember exactly.

210
00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:48.180
I think it was like honoring directors in the industry.

211
00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:49.280
And we were both there.

212
00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:53.760
And my friend came up to me and he said, I am so humiliated right now.

213
00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:55.320
And I said, what happened?

214
00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:01.380
And he goes, I just went up and bear hugged Amy Adams and said, Jenna, you look beautiful.

215
00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:02.620
Oh, no.

216
00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:03.980
Because he mixed us up.

217
00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:07.140
And people have this thing where they think she and I look a lot alike.

218
00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:09.840
We really don't look anything alike in person.

219
00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:13.120
Although I guess I just told a story about someone who mixed us up in person.

220
00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:14.680
I mean, there is a resemblance.

221
00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.160
She's perfectly cast because there is a resemblance.

222
00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:19.860
And you're both like beautiful ladies.

223
00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:20.460
Thanks.

224
00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:22.460
But I see a resemblance.

225
00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:23.780
I feel like we could play sisters.

226
00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:24.680
You could.

227
00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:25.720
All right, Hollywood.

228
00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:26.940
You heard it here firSt.

229
00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.640
I guess that's how I talk when I'm talking to Hollywood.

230
00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:30.620
I guess it is.

231
00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:31.900
Hollywood beware.

232
00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:34.740
Hollywood, this is Angela Kinsey.

233
00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:35.780
I don't love it.

234
00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:41.680
Your character, your Hollywood voice lady.

235
00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:42.600
I think you should work on it.

236
00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:43.020
Okay, thanks.

237
00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:43.780
Give it an accent.

238
00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:45.020
Should it be British?

239
00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:46.960
Hollywood, no.

240
00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:47.540
Oh, my God.

241
00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:49.580
Oh, that's Australian.

242
00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:51.380
Oh, what was that?

243
00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:53.300
That was like drunk Scottish.

244
00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:54.360
What is happening?

245
00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:55.720
I can't do accents.

246
00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:57.000
And I also can't sing.

247
00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:00.260
I can do Australian, but I grew up overseas and all my best friends were Aussies.

248
00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:02.960
So, right, now I'm just sitting here talking to Jenna.

249
00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:04.780
Jenna, we're going to talk about Hot Girl.

250
00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:05.260
Yeah.

251
00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:06.600
That's really good, Ange.

252
00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:07.160
Oh, thanks.

253
00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:08.260
Thanks so much.

254
00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:09.980
I'm shocked.

255
00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:11.580
I'm genuinely impressed.

256
00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:12.680
Okay, okay, okay.

257
00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:15.720
See, that was so good, but your Hollywood voice is a no.

